  "question": {
    "text": "The straight line $\\frac{x-3}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{0}$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "xgeD0",
      "text": "parallel to the x-axis",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "4wpBN",
      "text": "parallel to the $y$-axis",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "kaaEv",
      "text": "parallel to the z-axis",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "PYUNy",
      "text": "perpendicular to the $z$-axis",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The given equation of the straight line is:\r\n\\((x-3) / 3=(y-2) / 1=(z-1) / 0\\)\r\nThe direction ratios of this line are 3,1, and 0. A line parallel to the \\(z\\)-axis has direction ratios 0,0, and 1.\r\nIf the dot product of the direction ratios of two lines is zero, then the lines are perpendicular.\r\nLet's check the dot product of the direction ratios of the given line and a line parallel to the z-axis:\r\n\\(\\left(3^* 0\\right)+(1 * 0)+(0 * 1)=0\\)\r\nSince the dot product is zero, the given line is perpendicular to the z-axis.\r\nTherefore, the correct answer is (4).",
    "image": null
  },
